        "2. Actionable: Define exactly what the user/agent should do (e.g., 'buy 50 shares' vs. 'buy some shares');\n"
        "3. Rationale-backed: Tie rules to data from the Stock Assessment Report (e.g., 'aligns with analyst target price of $150' or 'uses 50-day MA support from technical analysis').\n"
        "\n"
        "Follow this Template for Each Stock's Trading Plan:\n"
        "1. [Stock Name] (Stock Code: [XXX])\n"
        "   (Pre-requisite: Confirm Stock Assessment Report for [XXX] is retrieved from MySQL; key reference points: [list 2–3 critical data points, e.g., '50-day MA = $142, analyst target = $160, Q4 earnings on Nov 20'])\n"
        "   \n"
        "   (a) Buying-in Rules\n"
        "   Each rule must specify Trigger condition (Data index threshold, event, or timeframe), Action (Exact quantity/amount to buy tied to position sizing from Stage I), and Rationale (Link to Stock Assessment Report data).\n"
        "   Buying-in Rule 1 (Language Description): ________ (e.g., 'If the stock's price drops to the 50-day moving average ($142) AND daily trading volume is at least 1.2x the 30-day average (≥5M shares) on or before Nov 15, 2025, buy 100 shares (equivalent to 8% of the user's portfolio, per Stage I position sizing). Rationale: Technical analysis in the Stock Assessment Report identifies $142 as a strong support level; sufficient volume ensures liquidity for entry.')\n"
        "   Buying-in Rule 2 (Language Description): ________ (e.g., 'If the stock reports Q4 earnings (scheduled Nov 20, 2025) with revenue growth ≥12% YoY (in line with analyst forecasts in the Report) AND the post-earnings price gap up is ≤5% (to avoid overpaying), buy 50 additional shares (to top up the position to the Stage I 8% allocation if Rule 1 was only partially filled). Rationale: Fundamental strength (earnings growth) confirms the stock’s value, while limiting gap-up risk aligns with the user's moderate risk tolerance.')\n"
        "   (Optional) Buying-in Rule 3 (Language Description): ________ (e.g., 'If the stock’s RSI drops to ≤35 (oversold condition, per technical indicators in the Report) AND the broader S&P 500 is not in a 5%+ weekly decline (to avoid market-wide downturns), buy 30 shares. Rationale: Oversold levels signal a potential price rebound, and market condition checks reduce systemic risk.')\n"
        "   \n"
        "   (b) Selling-out Rules\n"
        "   Each rule must specify Trigger condition (Profit threshold, loss threshold (stop-loss), event, or time limit), Action (Exact quantity/amount to sell or full position exit), and Rationale (Link to Stock Assessment Report data or user risk profile).\n"
        "   Selling-out Rule 1 (Language Description): ________ (e.g., 'If the stock’s price reaches the analyst target price of $160 (from the Stock Assessment Report) at any point before Mar 31, 2026, sell 100% of the position (150 shares). Rationale: $160 represents the consensus fair value, and locking in gains aligns with the user’s 6-month investment horizon.')\n"
        "   Selling-out Rule 2 (Language Description): ________ (e.g., 'If the stock’s price drops below the 200-day moving average ($130) OR declines by ≥10% from the average entry price (whichever comes first), sell 100% of the position. Rationale: The 200-day MA is a key long-term support level (per technical analysis in the Report); a 10% stop-loss limits losses to match the user’s maximum acceptable drawdown of 10%.')\n"
        "   (Optional) Selling-out Rule 3 (Language Description): ________ (e.g., 'If the stock misses Q1 2026 earnings forecasts (scheduled Feb 15, 2026) by ≥5% (revenue or EPS) OR announces a dividend cut (contrary to stable dividend projections in the Report), sell 100% of the position immediately. Rationale: Fundamental deterioration invalidates the original investment thesis, so exiting mitigates further downside.')\n"
        "   \n"
        "(Repeat the above template for each additional stock in the investment plan, adding more as needed)\n"
